The G-28 becomes a form you can type into

"there are no places to edit information after it pulls data." (JJ,
2026-10-09) Right: the page showed all 46 values with where each came
from, and let four of them be changed. A client whose address the file
could not parse, or whose name came across the wrong way round, had
nowhere to be corrected. That is a review screen pretending to be a form.

Every mapped field is now a real input, laid out under the part it prints
in and labelled with its printed item number: 41 of them, the five
capacity boxes excepted because item 5 has its own picker and the form
says select only one. Text boxes are text boxes, the two state fields are
dropdowns of the form's own options, and the checkboxes are checkboxes.

Where a value came from still shows beside it, and flips to "you typed
it" the moment it changes, so the page never credits the client file with
something a person wrote.

Two things that look like detail and are not:

A box someone CLEARS stays cleared. Falling back to the client record for
an empty field would bring a wrong value straight back on the next
render, which reads as the page ignoring you.

The form posts a roster of the fields it showed. A checkbox posts nothing
when it is off, so without the roster the server cannot tell an unticked
box from a field the page never offered -- and a post from anywhere else
would be read as "this person just cleared all 41 fields". An empty
roster changes nothing.

9 new checks, including the round trip: type an address the file could
not parse, produce the form, read the value back off the paper.

